PART 6 – Understanding Upstream Repository
6. What is Upstream?
This is another important concept.

Suppose Developer B has forked Developer A's repository.

Now Developer A makes new changes in the original repository.

Developer B wants to download those changes.

How can Developer B connect to the original repository?

We use another remote, conventionally called upstream.
Understand the architecture
        ORIGINAL REPOSITORY
         Developer A
                |
                |
             upstream
                |
                v
        FORKED REPOSITORY
         Developer B
                |
                |
              origin
                |
                v
         LOCAL COMPUTER

More precisely:
- origin refers to Developer B's fork.
- upstream refers to Developer A's original repository.
Practical 3: Configure Upstream
On Developer B's computer:
git remote add upstream https://github.com/DeveloperA/open-source-bakery.git

Check:
git remote -v

Expected:
origin    https://github.com/DeveloperB/open-source-bakery.git (fetch)
origin    https://github.com/DeveloperB/open-source-bakery.git (push)

upstream  https://github.com/DeveloperA/open-source-bakery.git (fetch)
upstream  https://github.com/DeveloperA/open-source-bakery.git (push)

Now Developer B has two remotes.
Step 1. Fetch original repository changes
git fetch upstream

This downloads the latest information from the original repository.
Step 2. Switch to main
git switch main

Step 3. Integrate upstream changes
git merge upstream/main

Step 4. Update your fork
git push origin main

Now Developer B's fork is synchronized with the original repository, assuming the merge completed successfully.

Important teaching point: Upstream is a conventional remote name. Git does not require the remote to be named upstream.
PART 7 – Understanding Pull Requests
7. What is a Pull Request?
Now we come to the heart of today's session.

Suppose Developer B has developed a new feature in their fork.

How can Developer B ask Developer A to include those changes in the original repository?

The answer is a Pull Request.

Definition:

A Pull Request (PR) is a GitHub collaboration mechanism through which a developer proposes changes to a repository and requests that those changes be reviewed and integrated.
Real-life analogy
Imagine a student preparing an assignment.

The student completes the assignment and submits it to the teacher.

The teacher:
1. Opens the assignment.
2. Reviews the content.
3. Suggests corrections.
4. Approves the assignment.
5. Accepts the final submission.
A Pull Request follows a similar process.

Pull Request workflow
Developer
    |
    v
Create Feature Branch
    |
    v
Write Code
    |
    v
Commit Changes
    |
    v
Push Feature Branch
    |
    v
Create Pull Request
    |
    v
Code Review
    |
    v
Approval / Changes Requested
    |
    v
Merge Pull Request
    |
    v
Main Branch Updated

Important distinction
A Pull Request is not the same as git pull.

Pull Request	Git Pull
| Pull Request | Git Pull |
| GitHub collaboration feature | Git command |
| Proposes changes for review | Retrieves and integrates remote changes |
| Supports comments and approvals | Does not provide a formal review process |
| Used in collaborative development | Used for repository synchronization |

