PART 9 – Understanding Code Review
8. What is Code Review?
Code Review is the process of examining another developer's code before integrating it into the main project.

Why is code review necessary?

Suppose Developer B submits code containing:
password = "admin123"


Or writes code with logical errors.

If the project owner merges the code without checking it, security and functionality problems may enter the application.

Code Review helps identify:
- Programming errors.
- Security vulnerabilities.
- Poor coding practices.
- Performance problems.
- Duplicate code.
- Missing documentation.
- Incorrect functionality.
Who performs code reviews?
Typically:
- Senior developers.
- Team leads.
- Project maintainers.
- Authorized reviewers.
Practical 5: Review a Pull Request
Student A opens the Pull Request created by Student B.

Navigate to:

Pull Requests → Select the Pull Request → Files changed

Student A can now inspect the code.

The reviewer can:
1. View changed files.
2. Examine added and removed lines.
3. Add comments.
4. Suggest improvements.
5. Approve changes.
6. Request changes.
Example review comment
Please add a navigation menu to products.html
so users can navigate back to the homepage.

Student B receives the feedback.

Student B modifies the file:
<nav>
    <a href="index.html">Home</a>
    <a href="products.html">Products</a>
</nav>

Then:
git add products.html
git commit -m "Added navigation menu"
git push

The existing Pull Request updates automatically because it tracks the pushed feature branch.

This is an important concept to demonstrate.

A Pull Request does not need to be recreated every time the developer makes corrections.
PART 10 – Approving and Merging Pull Requests
9. What does Approve mean?
When a reviewer is satisfied with the submitted code, they can approve the Pull Request.

GitHub provides different review outcomes.
Review option	Meaning
Comment	Provide feedback without approving or requesting changes
Approve	Accept the proposed changes
Request changes	Ask the contributor to make modifications


Practical 6: Approve the Pull Request
Student A:
1. Opens the Pull Request.
2. Selects the Files changed tab.
3. Clicks Review changes.
4. Selects Approve.
5. Submits the review.
Now Student A can merge the Pull Request, provided repository rules and required checks are satisfied.
Important: Merge Pull Request
GitHub commonly provides three merge strategies.
1. Create a Merge Commit
Creates a merge commit to combine the changes.

This preserves the branch integration history.
2. Squash and Merge
Combines all commits from the Pull Request into one commit on the base branch.

Useful when a feature branch contains many small development commits.
3. Rebase and Merge
Replays the commits onto the base branch without creating a traditional merge commit.

It produces a linear history.
Comparison
Strategy	Result
Merge Commit	Preserves branch history
Squash and Merge	Combines PR commits into one
Rebase and Merge	Replays commits onto base branch


After selecting the appropriate method, click:

Merge Pull Request → Confirm Merge

The changes are now integrated into Student A's main branch.
What happens next?
Student A's repository now contains:
fairy-bake-collaboration/
│
├── index.html
├── README.md
└── products.html

The Pull Request is merged.

This is how professional GitHub collaboration works.
PART 11 – Understanding GitHub Issues
10. What is a GitHub Issue?
Suppose users are reporting problems in your bakery application.

Examples:
- Homepage is not loading.
- Contact page is missing.
- Product images are not displaying.
- Login button is not working.
- Mobile layout is broken.
How can the development team record and manage these problems?

GitHub provides a feature called Issues.

Definition:

A GitHub Issue is used to track bugs, feature requests, improvements, tasks and discussions related to a repository.
What can we do with GitHub Issues?
1. Report bugs.
2. Request new features.
3. Assign tasks to developers.
4. Add labels.
5. Set milestones.
6. Discuss problems.
7. Reference commits and Pull Requests.
8. Track project progress.
