      PART 15 – Resolving Conflicts During Collaboration
Students already learned merge conflicts in Session 4.

Now explain how conflicts occur in the GitHub Pull Request workflow.
Scenario
Developer A changes:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake</h1>

Developer B changes:
<h1>Welcome to The Fairy Bake Bakery</h1>

Both modify the same line in separate branches.

Developer A merges their changes into main.

Developer B then creates a Pull Request using their outdated branch.

GitHub may indicate that the Pull Request has conflicts that must be resolved before merging.
How can Developer B resolve the conflict?
First, fetch the latest original repository changes:
git fetch upstream

Switch to the feature branch:
git switch feature-product-section

Rebase it onto the latest main:
git rebase upstream/main

If a conflict occurs:
git status

Open the conflicting file and resolve the conflict manually.

Then:
git add index.html
git rebase --continue

After a successful rebase, push the updated feature branch:
git push --force-with-lease origin feature-product-section

Important security and collaboration note: --force-with-lease rewrites the remote feature branch. Use it only when you own that branch or have coordinated with its contributors. It is safer than plain --force, but it is still a history-rewriting operation.

Alternatively, teams can merge the latest upstream main into the feature branch instead of rebasing.
